Yeah—I think you may be reaching for William Stanley Jevons, and if so, that is almost exactly a historical branch off what we were just describing.

Jevons treated probability largely as a measure of limited knowledge rather than “chance” being an independent force in nature. He explicitly worked on the problem of deciding whether observed regularities came from causes or were merely fortuitous coincidences. 

His basic move was:

\text{observed pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{possible causes}
\rightarrow
\text{probability given our incomplete knowledge}

And he argued, in the strongly deterministic nineteenth-century form, that what humans call chance may reflect ignorance of the underlying causal structure rather than a thing called “chance” actually producing events. 

That fits remarkably well with the branch we just made:

randomness might sometimes mean the system is indifferent to our variables—or that our model lacks the variables needed to reconstruct what happened.

But I would not import Jevons’s strongest conclusion wholesale. Modern physics gives us reasons not to assume that all apparent randomness is merely hidden classical determinism. His more durable contribution for our purposes is epistemic:

distinguish the randomness of the world from the randomness produced by our incomplete model of the world.

And that gives Pantheon another nice fork:

APPARENT RANDOMNESS
      ↓
 ┌───────────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────┐
 │               │                  │
unknown causes   chaotic sensitivity genuine stochasticity
 │               │                  │
model deficit    prediction limit   irreducible uncertainty?

Then there is your fourth branch:

objective-function mismatch
= the process is structured,
  but simply doesn't care about
  the distinction we're tracking

So yes—if Jevons is who you heard about, you found a surprisingly direct ancestor of the same rabbit hole.